Original commit body from 1bd1b62bb450913008b7a4de3f7e283b696a1207:

Design only; nothing in the pipeline changes. Records the owner's 2026-09-18
ruling on how two backends' records for one declaration are reconciled.

ADR-0012 §Step 3 described record-level coexistence: a higher-fidelity pass's
edges sit beside the AST pass's, at different confidence. Measured on a
two-arm Rust run (aardvark-dns, HYPERGUMBO_RUST_ANALYZER=1) that doubles every
callable and the in-crate call graph. The July "replace" sketch on WI-gojum is
lossy in both directions: 268 external-stub calls exist only on the
tree-sitter side, 79 field references only on the SCIP side.

ADR-0057 keeps ADR-0012's principle — both run, nothing is discarded — and
changes the unit: one node and one edge per declaration, each attribute a set
of (value, provenance) pairs (one value with two provenances on agreement, two
values on disagreement, one when only one producer saw it). Recognising the
pair is a declared merge pass at the top of Phase C, keyed on span containment
because both id and stable_id derive from the attributes that disagree. The
scalar a consumer reads comes from a per-record arbitration property with ONE
default, read from config.toml (ADR-0045 preferences tier; not a trust key).
The built-in is incumbent-first per attribute: SCIP's kind is wrong on 52/52
free functions, so "type-aware wins" is refused as the blanket.

Why a pass and not an on-demand facade: the merge key is whole-table, identity
is rewritten (296 .src/.dst and 345 .kind direct reads across 89 files), and
membership must settle before serialize (ADR-0043 R1/§8). Why not defer to
"presentation time": slice.py:828/838 and ranking.py:925/1044 branch on kind
and edge_type mid-traversal — that is consumption, not presentation.

